#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""mise-fleet-trust -- trust the mise configurations the fleet manifest opts in, and nothing else,
and write the mise settings that bound what mise reads.

    mise-fleet-trust [--base-source DIR] [--trust-file FILE]... [--dry-run]

WHY. mise applies a configuration's [env], settings and tasks only once that file is trusted, and
its trust records live in mise's state directory, which an agent's sandbox may not write. So trust is
decided in one place, the fleet manifest (`miseTrust: true` on a project, read through fleet-decl),
and recorded here, by the dotfiles apply, which runs outside any sandbox. Nothing else is trusted:
mise's own automatic trust (`mise exec`, `mise run`, `mise install` run outside a sandbox) and a
person's `mise trust` after reading a config are the only other ways in.

WHAT IT TRUSTS. For each project with `miseTrust: true` whose directory is on this machine, its
top-level `mise.toml` and `mise.local.toml`, each by its own path (`mise trust <file>`), never the
project's directory as a prefix: a prefix trusts every configuration below it, a dependency's
included. With --base-source DIR, also DIR/mise.toml, the dotfiles source's own; this needs no
manifest, so a machine with none still trusts it. With --trust-file FILE, also that one file, for
a task file a domain deploys outside any repository (home's CI bridge, on the CI node only); like
--base-source it needs no manifest. A file that does not exist is skipped, because
mise refuses to trust a path that is not there; the next run after it appears trusts it.

Measured with mise 2026.11.5: `mise trust <file>` records the file's DIRECTORY as a trust root, and
every configuration file in that directory (`.mise.toml`, `.config/mise/config.toml`, a later
`mise.local.toml`) counts as trusted with it, while a subdirectory's does not. Trusting each file by
its path is still the narrowest form mise offers outside paranoid mode's `config` category. That
record carries no content, and paranoid `env_files` does not change it: a configuration trusted this
way stays trusted after an edit. An environment file a configuration loads (a SOPS file) is trusted
separately and, under paranoid `env_files`, against its contents; this tool trusts none. mise runs
here with the machine's own configuration, so any paranoid setting comes from the deployed drop-ins.

THE MISERC. Two more settings complete the boundary: `ignored_config_paths` (every `node_modules`,
`.venv` and `vendor` directory at any depth inside each opted-in project, as the glob
`<dir>/**/node_modules/**`, and its `miseIgnore:` paths, so a dependency's configuration is never
read, and `mise exec` there never trusts and runs it) and `ceiling_paths`
(each project's parent, so the walk stops above the project). mise reads these before any
configuration file, so they take effect only from its "miserc", `miserc.toml` in mise's
configuration directory ($MISE_CONFIG_DIR, else $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mise, else ~/.config/mise), as
top-level keys, or from the environment; set in a conf.d/ drop-in, in config.toml or under
[settings] in the miserc, `mise settings get` reports them and they do nothing (measured, mise
2026.11.5). This tool owns that file and
writes it at every run, atomically, mode 644:

    # (a header naming this tool)
    ignored_config_paths = ["<dir>/**/.venv/**", "<dir>/**/node_modules/**", "<dir>/**/vendor/**",
                            "<dir>/fixtures"]
    ceiling_paths = ["<parent of dir>"]
    # (then a domain's own miserc settings, copied verbatim)

The domain's settings come from ~/.dotlocal/mise/miserc.extra.toml, when it exists: every other
setting mise reads only from the miserc goes there. A fragment that names either list is refused,
because the two are this tool's and a second assignment would make the file invalid or override
them. The dependency directories are listed whether or not they exist yet, and so are opted-in
projects not on disk here, so a clone or an `npm ci` after the apply is still covered. The
dependency globs are anchored at the project's directory and match at any depth below it, so a
workspace package's own `node_modules` is covered too; a `miseIgnore` entry is a plain path and
matches the directory it names and everything in it. Measured with mise 2026.11.5: the anchored glob
works from the miserc and from the environment, an unanchored `**/node_modules/**` matches nothing,
and a plain `<dir>/node_modules` misses `<dir>/pkg/node_modules`. The globs are per project, not one
`/**/node_modules/**` for the machine, because a global pattern would also ignore the configuration
of any project that sits under a directory of one of those names. A project whose directory itself
holds a glob character (`*`, `?`, `[`) is refused, because its glob would match other paths.

THE MANIFEST. Read through fleet-decl ($FLEET_RECORD, else ~/Devel/mani.yaml; a project's `path` is
relative to $FLEET_DEVEL_ROOT, else ~/Devel, or absolute). No manifest file means no project is
trusted and both lists are empty, which is not an error. A manifest fleet-decl cannot read, an
invalid `miseIgnore` entry (not a plain path inside its project) or a refused fragment stops the run
before any project is trusted and leaves the miserc as it was: an answer built from an unreadable
record would be unfounded, and a guessed ignore list could reach outside the project.
`fleet-decl --check` reports the same entries.

OPTIONS.
    --base-source DIR   also trust DIR/mise.toml (the apply passes the dotfiles source directory)
    --trust-file FILE   also trust FILE itself, by its path; repeatable
    --dry-run           print the miserc and what would be trusted; write and trust nothing

EXIT. 0 done (or nothing to do); 1 a `mise trust` failed or an opted-in project declares no path,
each named on stderr, the rest still done; 2 usage, mise not installed, fleet-decl not found, the
manifest unreadable, an invalid miseIgnore entry, a refused fragment, or the miserc not written.

Standard library only, Python 3.9 syntax.
"""
import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

PROG = "mise-fleet-trust"
CONFIG_NAMES = ("mise.toml", "mise.local.toml")
DEPENDENCY_DIRS = (".venv", "node_modules", "vendor")
GLOB_CHARS = "*?["
OWNED_KEYS = ("ignored_config_paths", "ceiling_paths")
HEADER = """\
# Generated by mise-fleet-trust at each dotfiles apply; an edit here is overwritten.
# mise reads this file (its "miserc") before any configuration file, and some settings, these
# among them, take effect only here or in the environment. The two lists below come from the fleet
# manifest's miseTrust projects; what follows them is ~/.dotlocal/mise/miserc.extra.toml, verbatim.
"""


class Refuse(Exception):
    """The run cannot proceed; exit 2."""


class Project(object):
    def __init__(self, name, directory, ignores):
        self.name = name
        self.dir = directory
        self.ignores = ignores


def say(msg):
    print("%s: %s" % (PROG, msg), file=sys.stderr)


def fleet_decl_command():
    """fleet-decl beside this file (installed, or the source copy), else on PATH."""
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
    for name in ("fleet-decl", "executable_fleet-decl"):
        candidate = os.path.join(here, name)
        if os.path.isfile(candidate) and os.access(candidate, os.X_OK):
            return [candidate]
    found = shutil.which("fleet-decl")
    if found:
        return [found]
    raise Refuse("fleet-decl is not installed, so the manifest cannot be read")


def record_path():
    return os.environ.get("FLEET_RECORD") or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), "Devel", "mani.yaml")


def decl_rows(fleet_decl, *args):
    """<project><TAB><value> rows of a bulk fleet-decl read; [] when nothing is declared."""
    out = subprocess.run(fleet_decl + list(args), stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
                         universal_newlines=True)
    if out.returncode == 1:
        return []
    if out.returncode != 0:
        detail = out.stderr.strip().splitlines()
        raise Refuse("the manifest %s could not be read (fleet-decl %s: exit %d%s)"
                     % (record_path(), args[0], out.returncode,
                        ", " + detail[-1] if detail else ""))
    rows = []
    for line in out.stdout.splitlines():
        name, sep, value = line.partition("\t")
        if sep:
            rows.append((name, value))
    return rows


def bad_ignore(entry):
    """Why a miseIgnore entry is refused, or None: the shape fleet-decl --check holds it to."""
    if not entry:
        return "is empty"
    if entry.startswith("/") or entry.startswith("~"):
        return "is not relative to the project"
    if any(part in ("", ".", "..") for part in entry.split("/")):
        return "has an empty, . or .. component"
    if any(c in entry for c in GLOB_CHARS):
        return "has a glob character"
    return None


def project_dir(raw):
    if raw.startswith("/"):
        joined = raw
    else:
        root = os.environ.get("FLEET_DEVEL_ROOT") or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), "Devel")
        joined = os.path.join(root, raw)
    # Resolved where it exists, so the paths match the ones mise sees from a working directory.
    return os.path.realpath(joined) if os.path.isdir(joined) else os.path.normpath(joined)


def load_projects():
    """(projects, errors): every opted-in project, on disk or not. Raises Refuse."""
    if not os.path.isfile(record_path()):
        say("no fleet record at %s: no project is trusted" % record_path())
        return [], 0
    fleet_decl = fleet_decl_command()
    opted = [name for name, value in decl_rows(fleet_decl, "--values", "miseTrust")
             if value == "true"]
    if not opted:
        return [], 0
    paths = dict(decl_rows(fleet_decl, "--values", "path"))
    ignores = {}
    for name, entry in decl_rows(fleet_decl, "--values-list", "miseIgnore"):
        ignores.setdefault(name, []).append(entry)
    for name in opted:
        for entry in ignores.get(name, []):
            why = bad_ignore(entry)
            if why:
                raise Refuse("%s: miseIgnore entry '%s' %s (fleet-decl --check reports it too)"
                             % (name, entry, why))
    projects, errors = [], 0
    for name in opted:
        raw = paths.get(name, "")
        if not raw:
            say("%s: opts in to mise trust but declares no path; skipped" % name)
            errors += 1
            continue
        directory = project_dir(raw)
        if any(c in directory for c in GLOB_CHARS):
            raise Refuse("%s: its directory %s holds a glob character, so its ignore globs would "
                         "match other paths; move or rename it" % (name, directory))
        projects.append(Project(name, directory, ignores.get(name, [])))
    return projects, errors


def ignore_paths(project):
    paths = [os.path.join(project.dir, "**", d, "**") for d in DEPENDENCY_DIRS]
    paths += [os.path.join(project.dir, entry) for entry in project.ignores]
    return paths


def ceiling_paths(project):
    return [os.path.dirname(project.dir)]


def mise_config_dir():
    """mise's configuration directory, found as mise finds it."""
    if os.environ.get("MISE_CONFIG_DIR"):
        return os.environ["MISE_CONFIG_DIR"]
    config = os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME") or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".config")
    return os.path.join(config, "mise")


def fragment_path():
    return os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".dotlocal", "mise", "miserc.extra.toml")


def read_fragment():
    """The domain's miserc settings, verbatim, or "" when there are none. Raises Refuse when the
    fragment cannot be read or names a setting this tool owns."""
    path = fragment_path()
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        return ""
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            text = f.read()
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
        raise Refuse("the miserc fragment %s cannot be read: %s" % (path, e))
    for number, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
        code = line.split("#", 1)[0]
        for key in OWNED_KEYS:
            if re.search(r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9_-])%s(?![A-Za-z0-9_-])" % key, code):
                raise Refuse("the miserc fragment %s sets %s (line %d): this tool writes that list "
                             "from the fleet manifest, so a domain cannot set it too; extend it "
                             "with miseIgnore or the manifest instead" % (path, key, number))
    if text and not text.endswith("\n"):
        text += "\n"
    return text


def toml_list(paths):
    # A JSON string is a valid TOML basic string: the same quoting and escapes.
    return "[" + ", ".join(json.dumps(p) for p in paths) + "]"


def miserc_text(projects, fragment):
    ignored, ceilings = set(), set()
    for project in projects:
        ignored.update(ignore_paths(project))
        ceilings.update(ceiling_paths(project))
    text = (HEADER + "ignored_config_paths = " + toml_list(sorted(ignored)) + "\n"
            + "ceiling_paths = " + toml_list(sorted(ceilings)) + "\n")
    if fragment:
        text += "\n# --- %s ---\n" % fragment_path() + fragment
    try:
        import tomllib  # Python 3.11 and later; where it is missing, mise is the only parser
    except ImportError:
        return text
    try:
        tomllib.loads(text)
    except tomllib.TOMLDecodeError as e:
        raise Refuse("the miserc would not parse (%s); is the fragment %s valid TOML?"
                     % (e, fragment_path()))
    return text


def write_miserc(text):
    """Replace mise's miserc in one step: a reader sees the old file or the new one, never part."""
    directory = mise_config_dir()
    target = os.path.join(directory, "miserc.toml")
    try:
        os.makedirs(directory, exist_ok=True)
        fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=".miserc.", dir=directory)
        try:
            with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write(text)
            os.chmod(tmp, 0o644)
            os.replace(tmp, target)
        except BaseException:
            os.unlink(tmp)
            raise
    except OSError as e:
        raise Refuse("cannot write %s: %s" % (target, e))
    print("wrote %s" % target)


def trust_file(mise, path, dry_run):
    """Trust one existing file by its path. Returns False when mise refused."""
    if dry_run:
        print("would trust %s" % path)
        return True
    out = subprocess.run([mise, "trust", "--quiet", path], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                         stderr=subprocess.PIPE, universal_newlines=True)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        detail = out.stderr.strip().splitlines()
        say("mise trust %s failed (exit %d)%s" % (path, out.returncode,
                                                  ": " + detail[0] if detail else ""))
        return False
    print("trusted %s" % path)
    return True


def trust_project(mise, project, dry_run):
    """Trust the project's own top-level configuration files. Returns False on any failure."""
    ok = True
    for name in CONFIG_NAMES:
        path = os.path.join(project.dir, name)
        if os.path.isfile(path):
            ok = trust_file(mise, path, dry_run) and ok
    return ok


def main(argv):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=PROG, add_help=True,
                                     description="Trust the mise configurations the fleet "
                                                 "manifest opts in, and write mise's miserc "
                                                 "(see the header).")
    parser.add_argument("--base-source", metavar="DIR")
    parser.add_argument("--trust-file", metavar="FILE", action="append", default=[])
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    try:
        mise = shutil.which("mise")
        if not mise:
            raise Refuse("mise is not installed")
        failures = 0
        # The base source first: it depends on no manifest, so an unreadable one does not stop it.
        if args.base_source:
            path = os.path.join(os.path.realpath(args.base_source), "mise.toml")
            if os.path.isfile(path):
                failures += 0 if trust_file(mise, path, args.dry_run) else 1
            else:
                say("no mise.toml in %s; nothing to trust there" % args.base_source)
        for name in args.trust_file:
            path = os.path.realpath(name)
            if os.path.isfile(path):
                failures += 0 if trust_file(mise, path, args.dry_run) else 1
            else:
                say("%s does not exist; nothing to trust there" % name)
        # Everything is read and checked before anything about the projects is written.
        projects, errors = load_projects()
        text = miserc_text(projects, read_fragment())
        failures += errors
        if args.dry_run:
            print("would write %s:" % os.path.join(mise_config_dir(), "miserc.toml"))
            sys.stdout.write(text)
        else:
            write_miserc(text)
        for project in projects:
            if not os.path.isdir(project.dir):
                say("%s: %s is not on disk here; skipped" % (project.name, project.dir))
                continue
            failures += 0 if trust_project(mise, project, args.dry_run) else 1
    except Refuse as e:
        say("%s -- no project was trusted, and the miserc is unchanged" % e)
        return 2
    return 1 if failures else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
